#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""verify-scope — release-shipped verification-scope reporter.

    workflows/_shared/scripts/verify-scope [--base <rev>] [--full | --micro | --light] [--json]
                                           [--run] [--since <rev>]

The report is written on stdout and nothing else. It covers the diff from
a base revision to HEAD. The default report is line-oriented:

    mode: full|scoped
    path: <repo-relative path>      one per affected path, sorted, unique
    command: <command>              the commands to run, in the fixed order

``--json`` replaces the line report with exactly one JSON object (``schema:
1``, the contract in docs/contributing.md "Verification tiers"): the same
mode, paths and commands — each command with its name, phase
(fast|full|scoped, or micro under --micro), declared prerequisites,
declared timeout (null when none is declared, so the consumer's own default
applies), declared failure report ({format, path}, null when none is
declared) and source (conf|default) — plus the stack, the analyzer, whether
the conf is present and whether the diff changed it, the resolved base/head
shas and default branch, the conf's test_glob, base_setup, runner,
e2e_runner, suite_glob and e2e_map declarations and every stderr diagnostic;
a repository that declares smoke_suite= also gets ``smoke_suite`` and the
full-tier e2e selection ``e2e_selection`` (null unless one was attempted;
see "Full-tier e2e selection" below); one with an accepted host_requires=
line also gets ``host_requires``, its host prerequisites ``[{name, probe,
hint}]`` in file order (absent otherwise).

The engine itself is a Python 3 program (a documented ``pf doctor``
prerequisite): the shell implementation it replaces is gone, and no
forwarding stub remains. The conf, the CLI and the report are byte-for-byte
the shell engine's (the contract is docs/contributing.md, "Verification
tiers").

``--micro`` is the micro lane's evidence mode: the conf is read as it is at
the diff base (``git show <base>:.polyforge/verify-scope.conf``, never the
working tree, so a change cannot widen its own declaration; an absent or
unreadable base conf declares nothing and is diagnosed), ``mode`` reads
``micro``, and ``commands`` is exactly the fast phase followed by the
conf's micro_check= commands (phase ``micro``, named micro-<i> in file
order) — no scoped or full command. With ``--run`` those run in order from
the repository root, stopping at the first non-zero; under ``--micro`` a
missing declared prerequisite, a repository that declares no micro_path= at
the base, or an empty command list is a failure, never a skip.

``--light`` is the develop lane's light tier: the report is computed like
the default one, except that the conf's own full-tier triggers do not
apply — a forced_full= match and the module_root= spanning rule force
nothing — a changed path no carries: declaration covers is diagnosed (the
fast gates still cover it) instead of forcing the full tier, and at a
nested engine root a changed path outside the root is left to the enclosing
root's run (diagnosed, and dropped from ``paths``). The analyzer's own
fallbacks still answer full: a stack config file (go.mod, package.json, a
lockfile, …), an unbuildable package graph (an embed pattern matching no
file — ``//go:embed all:dist`` before a front-end build — does not make it
unbuildable: it changes no package's dependencies), a structural carries
diagnostic, no analyzer. Under --json the object gains ``light: true``; a
report without --light keeps its key set. --light cannot be combined with
--full or --micro (exit 2). The full tier's forced-full rules stay the full
gate's (a later integration run), which catches what the light tier leaves
out.

``--since <rev>`` adds one query to the report: whether the changes from
<rev> (a head verified earlier) to HEAD — the upstream a delivery merged in
— touch the affected set of this diff (base..HEAD). It is symmetric: each
side's affected set is computed the same way (Go: the changed packages,
their reverse-dependency closure — the go_harness package kept in it — the
packages whose tests name a changed path, the declared go_harness;
npm/python/rust: the carries-selected suites; any stack: the changed paths
themselves) and the answer is whether they meet, so an upstream change to
a dependency of this diff's packages meets it as well as one to a
dependent. A side changing a forced-full path, a path in the go_harness
directory, a path outside a nested engine root or (carries) a path no
declaration covers affects everything; an upstream change to the conf, a
malformed carries key or declaration, no analyzer, an unresolvable <rev>,
an unreadable diff or an unbuildable package graph answer intersects: true
(fail closed). Under --json the object gains ``since: {head, intersects,
paths, reason}`` (head the resolved sha, paths what both sides reach); the
line report ends with ``since: <sha> intersects=<bool>``, one
``since-path:`` per path and ``since-reason:``. Without --since the report
is unchanged.

``--base <rev>`` overrides the default base. The default base is ``git
merge-base HEAD origin/<default>``, where <default> is the branch
``origin/HEAD`` points at (fallback ``main``) — or, when the engine runs
inside a step (``PF_CAPSULE`` names the capsule), the capsule's fixed
target branch (``runtime-state/target-branch.json``, a sub-ticket's)
stands in for ``<default>``; a file that is there but cannot be read is
diagnosed and answers the full tier, never a fall-back to the default
branch. The capsule baseline in ``capsule.json`` is NEVER read. ``--full`` forces the full tier whatever
the diff; it never narrows anything (a caller verifying a full-tier work
item uses it to get the full command list).

The target repository is the current working directory (the engine root).
A nested project root — a subdirectory of a git work tree with its own
conf, such as ``cd pi-web`` — is a repository of its own: git names a
diff's paths from the work tree's top, so the engine makes them relative to
the engine root, like every other path it reads (the conf's globs, the
carries declarations, the listed files). A changed path outside the engine
root is reported as ``../<path>``; no declaration of the root can account
for it, so it falls back to the full tier (and the full-tier e2e selection
to the full tail), diagnosed. At the work tree's top nothing changes.
Diagnostics go to stderr. ``--run`` prints the report and then executes
the reported commands in order with ``bash -c`` from the repository root
(stdin closed), stopping at the first non-zero and exiting non-zero; a
command whose declared prerequisites (an unset or empty check_requires_env
variable, a check_requires_cmd executable not on PATH) are missing is
skipped with a stderr notice, and timeouts are reported, not enforced.
Their output goes to stderr so stdout keeps the machine-readable shape.

Conf first: the repository's declared commands are reported for every
stack. Stack detection: go.mod selects the Go analyzer, package.json the
npm analyzer, pyproject.toml or requirements.txt the python analyzer and
Cargo.toml the rust analyzer; a ``stack=`` pin overrides detection and
``stack=none`` pins "no analyzer". With no analyzer — ``stack=none``, an
unsupported stack (no supported manifest), an ambiguous one (two or more
supported manifests without a pin) or an npm manifest whose scripts cannot
be read — the report is ``mode: full`` with the conf's commands only (none
without a conf). A scope that cannot be computed always falls back to
``full``, never to a wrong ``scoped``.

A repository may declare a declarative ``.polyforge/verify-scope.conf``
(line-oriented; ``#`` comments and blank lines ignored; a value runs to
the end of its line, so there is no trailing comment on a value line). The
key list is docs/reference/config.md's conf reference; the shell engine's
header comment carried it verbatim and this rewrite changes none of it.

An empty or malformed e2e_runner=, suite_glob=, e2e_map= or smoke_suite=
value (e2e_map= split by exactly one ``=``; smoke_suite= one relative path
without blanks) is diagnosed and forces the full tier.

Full-tier e2e selection: with --full, at least one smoke_suite= line and a
non-empty diff, the full tier's e2e phase is not the full tail but the
carries-selected suites union the smoke suites (``e2e_selection.mode``
carries), or the smoke suites alone when every changed path is
documentation or an exclude= match (mode smoke); a changed
non-documentation path no declaration covers is listed in
``e2e_selection.uncovered_paths`` and does not force the full tail. A
forced-full match, a changed path outside a nested engine root, a change to
this conf, or a structural diagnostic (a malformed carries or smoke_suite=
key, an analyzer without the carries layer, an undeclared or malformed
suite, an unresolvable import, an e2e_map= or smoke_suite= naming no
discovered suite, an empty full tail) runs the full tail (mode full) and is
diagnosed. ``mode:`` stays the tier fact (full), and
``e2e_selection.full_commands`` always carries the full tail, the bar a
delivery re-runs. The scoped tier never adds a smoke suite.

Any fast-phase line (fast_gate= or check=<name>|fast|…) replaces the
analyzer's default fast gates, and any full-phase line replaces the default
full tail; the anonymous lines are numbered per phase from 1 in file
order. A check name is letters, digits, ``.``, ``_`` and ``-``, starts with
a letter or digit, is declared once, and never has the form fast-<n>,
full-<n>, scoped-<n> or micro-<n> (the anonymous and analyzer commands'
names), nor audit or audit-<anything> (the dependency-audit gate's). A
check_*, report= or rerun= line names a declared conf command (a check=
name, or fast-<i>/full-<i>/micro-<i>); a line naming none is diagnosed and
ignored, as is a malformed one.

The conf is declarative: it is never evaluated at parse time and it has no
hook-style keys (pre/post/exec); an unknown key is diagnosed and contributes
nothing — except the keys another tool owns (``not_runnable_rule=``, the
replay judge's, and ``component=`` / ``epic_components=`` / ``epic_units=`` /
``epic_files=``, the component tool's, polyforge#89): the engine reads none
of them, so it accepts their lines without the diagnostic. A command value
is an opaque string: it is reported as a
``command:`` line and, only with --run, executed by ``bash -c`` (command
values are bash), with the same trust as the repository's own Makefile. A
command value whose first characters are ``$(`` or a backtick is skipped
with a lint diagnostic; that check is a lint convenience, not a security
boundary (a value such as ``true; touch x`` is a normal command and is not
inspected).

Dependency-audit gate: every full-tier report of a repository with an
analyzer ends in one engine-appended command named ``audit`` (phase full,
source default, no prerequisites, null timeout), the audit-deps helper
shipped beside this engine over the repository's auditable surfaces — the
root go.mod (--go) and every package-lock.json/npm-shrinkwrap.json outside
node_modules/ (--npm <dir>, sorted); a repository with none gets no audit.
It follows the conf's full lines and the carries selection, ends
e2e_selection.full_commands, and exactly one conf key reaches it —
``audit_prod_level=`` (polyforge#113), which only tightens: a declared
``moderate`` or ``low`` appends `` --npm-prod-level <level>`` to the command
(the helper then runs each npm surface's production-only audit at that
level too), while an empty value, one that does not tighten (high, critical,
anything else) or a second declaration is diagnosed and ignored and the
command stays byte-identical — a repository that declares none is
unaffected, and an engine that predates the key diagnoses it as unknown.
Every other key stays out of its reach: ``audit`` and ``audit-*`` are
reserved check names, a check_* or report= line cannot bind
to it and exclude= does not narrow its surfaces. At a nested engine root
(below the top of the git work tree) it names the repository's one ledger,
the top's .polyforge/audit-exemptions.conf, as --ledger relative to the
root (one ``../`` per segment); at the top the helper's default already is
that file. Scoped, --micro and no-analyzer reports carry none. The gate's
own contract is in audit-deps (beside it).

Zero-config defaults are stack-generic. A Go repo's top-level test/
directory is an ordinary package unless the conf declares
``go_harness=<path>`` (for example ``go_harness=test/e2e-parallel.sh``): the
engine then keeps the harness's directory out of the Go package graph and
runs the harness for the tests whose source references a changed path; the
Go analyzer never reads carries: headers. The npm, python and rust
analyzers select e2e suites by their carries: declarations (the carries
layer, described below). The npm fast gates come from the scripts
package.json actually declares (lint, test, and a TypeScript typecheck only
when TypeScript is configured); the full tier reports every declared script
that runs an e2e/*.mjs suite and every remaining suite. The python analyzer
reports ``python -m compileall -q``, the unit tests that import a changed
module, and ``pytest`` in full; the rust analyzer reports
``cargo build --all-targets``, the unit tests of the modules that contain
or use a changed module, and ``cargo test`` in full. Either falls back to
full when a changed module's tests cannot be resolved.
Repository-specific commands belong in the conf.

The carries layer is stack-neutral. A suite declares the repository paths
it covers, relative to the engine root, in its opening comment block — the
leading blank and comment lines (``//`` or ``#``) before the first line of
code:

    // carries: components/Panel*, lib/panel*

Several carries: lines union; globs are separated by commas or blanks and
use the shell-case syntax forced_full= and exclude= use (``*`` crosses
``/``). A carries: line after the first code line is not a declaration.
Suites are the analyzer's default discovery — npm e2e/*.mjs, python
test_*.py and *_test.py under e2e/ or tests/e2e/, rust top-level tests/*.rs
— or the conf's suite_glob= globs, minus exclude= matches and the
e2e_runner= target; a discovered file that another module imports and that
declares nothing is a helper, not a suite. Selected suites run as one
e2e_runner= command, else one ``node <suite>``, ``pytest <suite>`` or
``cargo test --test <name>`` each. e2e_map=<glob>=<suite> replaces that
suite's declaration outright. The layer is on only when at least one suite
exists. Selection: every suite whose globs match a changed path, a changed
suite itself, and every suite that (transitively) imports a changed
helper. Any undeclared or malformed suite, an unresolvable import, an
e2e_map= naming no suite, or a changed path that is neither
documentation, an exclude= match, a suite, a helper nor covered by a
declaration falls back to the full tier (the full-tier e2e selection lists
such a path instead; see the opening contract).

The carries: header parse is pfstep's one implementation
(``pfstep/carries.py`` — parse_header), shared with the step scripts, and
the shell-case globs are pfstep's too (``pfstep/goglob.py`` — case_match).

This is a verification reporter, not a release or deploy entry point.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Sequence, Set, Tuple

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

from pfstep import targetbranch  # noqa: E402
from pfstep.carries import parse_header  # noqa: E402
from pfstep.goglob import case_match  # noqa: E402

# us is the ASCII unit separator: the field separator of the engine's own
# record files (a conf value is one line, so it never carries one in
# practice).
US = "\x1f"  # noqa: F841 — kept for the record format's documentation

# The POSIX C locale's [[:space:]] (what the shell engine's sed classes
# matched).
C_SPACE = " \t\n\v\f\r"

# A positive Go duration (time.ParseDuration's grammar without a sign).
DUR_RE = re.compile(r"^(([0-9]+(\.[0-9]*)?|\.[0-9]+)(ns|us|ms|s|m|h))+$", re.ASCII)

USAGE = """usage: workflows/_shared/scripts/verify-scope [--base <rev>] [--full | --micro | --light] [--json] [--run] [--since <rev>]
  --base <rev>   diff base (default: merge-base HEAD origin/<default>)
  --full         force the full tier whatever the diff
  --micro        micro-lane evidence: the base conf, fast phase + micro_check
  --light        the light tier: the conf's forced-full triggers do not apply
  --json         print the report as one JSON object (needs python3)
  --run          print the report, then run the reported commands in order
  --since <rev>  also report whether <rev>..HEAD touches this diff's affected set
"""


def c_lstrip(s: str) -> str:
    return s.lstrip(C_SPACE)


def c_rstrip(s: str) -> str:
    return s.rstrip(C_SPACE)


def path_dir(p: str) -> str:
    """The repo-relative directory of a repo-relative path."""
    d = p.rsplit("/", 1)[0] if "/" in p else p
    return "." if d == p else d


def rel_dir(root: str, d: str) -> str:
    """An absolute package directory mapped back to the repo root."""
    if d == root:
        return "."
    if d.startswith(root + "/"):
        return d[len(root) + 1:]
    return d


def join_pipe(items: Sequence[str]) -> str:
    """The lines joined by `|` (input pre-sorted)."""
    return "|".join(items)


def sq(s: str) -> str:
    """One shell single-quoted word."""
    return "'" + s.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


def cut300(s: str) -> str:
    """cut -c1-300."""
    return s[:300]


def awk_num(s: str) -> int:
    """awk's `$n + 0`: a non-numeric string reads 0."""
    try:
        return int(s)
    except ValueError:
        return 0


class Engine:
    def __init__(self, argv: List[str]) -> None:
        self.root = os.getcwd()
        self.engine_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
        self.tmp = ""
        # Parsed arguments.
        self.base_arg = ""
        self.since_arg = ""
        self.since_set = False
        self.run = False
        self.json = False
        self.force_full = False
        self.micro = False
        self.light = False
        self.parse_args(argv)
        self.diag_lines: List[str] = []
        # One command record: [name, phase, source, cmd] (name "" = an
        # analyzer-default command, named <phase>-<i> once the list is
        # complete).
        self.commands: List[List[str]] = []
        self.scoped_cmds: List[str] = []
        # Conf state (file order).
        self.conf_fast: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_full: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_forced: List[str] = []
        self.conf_roots: List[str] = []
        self.conf_excl: List[str] = []
        self.conf_stack: List[str] = []
        self.conf_harness: List[str] = []
        self.conf_names: List[str] = []
        self.conf_req_env: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_req_cmd: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_timeout: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_report: List[Tuple[str, ...]] = []
        self.conf_rerun: List[Tuple[str, ...]] = []
        self.conf_globs: List[str] = []
        self.conf_base_setup: List[str] = []
        self.conf_suite_glob: List[str] = []
        self.conf_e2e_map: List[str] = []
        self.conf_smoke: List[str] = []
        self.conf_micro_path: List[str] = []
        self.conf_micro: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.conf_host: List[Tuple[str, ...]] = []
        self.conf_runner = ""
        self.conf_e2e_runner = ""
        self.conf_e2e_start_threshold = ""
        self.conf_e2e_start_prefix = ""
        # polyforge#113: the one conf key that reaches the audit gate, and
        # only tightens — the npm production threshold below the default high.
        self.conf_audit_prod_level = ""
        self.anon_fast = 0
        self.anon_full = 0
        self.anon_micro = 0
        # conf_closed is set when a carries key (e2e_runner=, suite_glob=,
        # e2e_map=) is empty or malformed: the report then falls back to
        # the full tier, never to a narrower scope computed as if the key
        # were absent.
        self.conf_closed = False
        # smoke_declared is set by any smoke_suite= line, valid or not.
        self.smoke_declared = False
        self.analyzer = ""
        self.stack = ""
        self.npm_scripts: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.go_harness = ""
        self.go_harness_dir = ""
        self.full = 0
        self.diff_ok = False
        self.default_branch = "main"
        self.base = ""
        self.base_sha = ""
        self.head_sha = ""
        self.root_prefix = ""
        self.changed: List[str] = []
        self.outside: List[str] = []
        self.conf_state = "absent"
        self.conf_changed = "false"
        self.mode = "full"
        self.sel_mode = ""
        self.sel_suites: List[str] = []
        self.sel_uncov: List[str] = []
        self.sel_full: List[str] = []
        # Carries layer state.
        self.cr_files: List[str] = []
        self.cr_cand: List[str] = []
        self.cr_runner: List[str] = []
        self.cr_status: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.cr_decl: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.cr_nodes: Set[str] = set()
        self.cr_edges: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.cr_unres: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.cr_suites: List[str] = []
        self.cr_maperr: List[str] = []
        self.py_edges: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        self.rs_edges: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        # Go analyzer state.
        self.go_graph_state = ""
        self.go_graph_why = ""
        self.packages: List[Tuple[str, str, str]] = []
        self.pkg_dirs: Set[str] = set()
        # --since state.
        self.since_head = ""
        self.since_intersects = "true"
        self.since_reason = ""
        self.since_hits: List[str] = []

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Argument parsing.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def parse_args(self, argv: List[str]) -> None:
        i = 0
        while i < len(argv):
            a = argv[i]
            if a == "--base":
                if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                    self.usage_err("--base needs a revision")
                self.base_arg = argv[i + 1]
                i += 2
            elif a.startswith("--base="):
                self.base_arg = a[len("--base="):]
                if self.base_arg == "":
                    self.usage_err("--base needs a revision")
                i += 1
            elif a == "--since":
                if i + 1 >= len(argv) or argv[i + 1] == "":
                    self.usage_err("--since needs a revision")
                self.since_arg = argv[i + 1]
                self.since_set = True
                i += 2
            elif a.startswith("--since="):
                self.since_arg = a[len("--since="):]
                if self.since_arg == "":
                    self.usage_err("--since needs a revision")
                self.since_set = True
                i += 1
            elif a == "--run":
                self.run = True
                i += 1
            elif a == "--json":
                self.json = True
                i += 1
            elif a == "--full":
                self.force_full = True
                i += 1
            elif a == "--micro":
                self.micro = True
                i += 1
            elif a == "--light":
                self.light = True
                i += 1
            elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
                sys.stderr.write(USAGE)
                sys.exit(0)
            else:
                self.usage_err("unknown argument '%s'" % a)
        for other, set_flag in (("full", self.force_full), ("micro", self.micro)):
            if self.light and set_flag:
                sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: --light cannot be combined with --%s\n" % other)
                sys.stderr.write(USAGE)
                sys.exit(2)

    def usage_err(self, msg: str) -> None:
        sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: %s\n" % msg)
        sys.stderr.write(USAGE)
        sys.exit(2)

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Child processes and diagnostics.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def child_env(self, extra: Optional[Dict[str, str]] = None) -> Dict[str, str]:
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["LC_ALL"] = "C"
        if extra:
            env.update(extra)
        return env

    def proc(self, argv: List[str], cwd: Optional[str] = None,
            env: Optional[Dict[str, str]] = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
        return subprocess.run(argv, cwd=cwd, env=env or self.child_env(),
                              stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)

    def diag(self, msg: str) -> None:
        line = "verify-scope: %s" % msg
        sys.stderr.write(line + "\n")
        self.diag_lines.append(line)

    def diff_base_branch(self) -> Optional[str]:
        """The branch the default diff base is computed from when --base
        names none: the step capsule's fixed target branch when
        PF_CAPSULE names a capsule carrying one (a step context; outside
        one — `pf doctor`, a hand run — the variable is absent and the
        answer is the default branch's), else the branch origin/HEAD
        points at (fallback main). None = the fixed target cannot be
        read: the engine diagnoses it and never falls back to the default
        branch behind a sub-ticket's fixed target (the full tier
        answers)."""
        capsule = os.environ.get("PF_CAPSULE", "")
        if capsule:
            try:
                target = targetbranch.read(capsule)
            except targetbranch.TargetBranchError as err:
                self.diag("%s — the base falls back to nothing: the full tier answers, never the default branch" % err)
                return None
            if target:
                return target
        c = self.proc(["git", "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"], cwd=self.root)
        default = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() if c.returncode == 0 else ""
        default = default[len("origin/"):] if default.startswith("origin/") else default
        return default or "main"

    def read_text(self, path: str) -> str:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            return f.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Helpers over the conf's shell-case globs and path classes.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def path_excluded(self, p: str) -> bool:
        base = p.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        for g in self.conf_excl:
            if g == "":
                continue
            if case_match(g, p) or case_match(g, base):
                return True
        return False

    def is_doc_path(self, p: str) -> bool:
        if p.endswith((".md", ".markdown", ".rst", ".txt")):
            return True
        return p.startswith("docs/") or p.startswith("doc/")

    def path_forced_conf(self, p: str) -> bool:
        base = p.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        for g in self.conf_forced:
            if g == "":
                continue
            if case_match(g, p) or case_match(g, base):
                return True
        return False

    def path_forced_stack(self, p: str) -> bool:
        base = p.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        a = self.analyzer
        if a == "go":
            return p in ("go.mod", "go.sum")
        if a == "npm":
            if p in ("package.json", "package-lock.json", "npm-shrinkwrap.json", "tsconfig.json"):
                return True
            if base.startswith("tsconfig.") or base == "tsconfig.json":
                return True
            if base.startswith("eslint.config.") or base == "eslint.config.json":
                return True
            if base == ".eslintrc" or base.startswith(".eslintrc."):
                return True
        if a == "python":
            if p in ("pyproject.toml", "setup.py", "setup.cfg", "pytest.ini", "tox.ini"):
                return True
            if p.startswith("requirements") and p.endswith(".txt"):
                return True
            if base == "conftest.py":
                return True
        if a == "rust":
            if p in ("Cargo.toml", "Cargo.lock", "build.rs", "rust-toolchain", "rust-toolchain.toml"):
                return True
            if p.startswith(".cargo/"):
                return True
        return False

    def path_forced(self, p: str) -> bool:
        return self.path_forced_stack(p) or self.path_forced_conf(p)

    def root_up(self) -> str:
        """The path from the engine root back to the top of the git work
        tree: one `../` per segment of root_prefix."""
        return "../" * self.root_prefix.count("/")

    def inside_work_tree(self) -> bool:
        c = self.proc(["git", "rev-parse", "--is-inside-work-tree"], cwd=self.root)
        return c.returncode == 0 and c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() == "true"

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The declarative conf. Every key is read without evaluation; a
    # rejected line is diagnosed on stderr and contributes nothing.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def conf_cmd_ok(self, key: str, val: str, line: str) -> bool:
        """Whether a command value (key, value, line) is reportable; a
        rejected value is diagnosed and contributes nothing."""
        if val.startswith("$(") or val.startswith("`"):
            self.diag("conf lint: %s value begins with a command substitution (lint diagnostic only;"
                      " command values run as reported command: lines): %s" % (key, line))
            return False
        if val == "":
            self.diag("conf ignores an empty %s" % key)
            return False
        return True

    @staticmethod
    def rerun_unit_quoted(tmpl: str) -> bool:
        """Whether a rerun= template places {unit} inside single or double
        quotes (a backslash outside single quotes escapes the next
        character): the unit name is shell-quoted where {unit} stands, so
        inside quotes the quotes themselves would reach the command."""
        q = ""
        i = 0
        n = len(tmpl)
        while i < n:
            c = tmpl[i]
            if q != "'" and c == "\\":
                i += 2
                continue
            if tmpl.startswith("{unit}", i) and q != "":
                return True
            if q == "" and c in ("'", '"'):
                q = c
            elif q != "" and c == q:
                q = ""
            i += 1
        return False

    @staticmethod
    def conf_items(kind: str, val: str) -> Optional[List[str]]:
        """A comma-separated list split into items (None when the list is
        empty or any item is not of its class)."""
        items = val.split(",")
        for it in items:
            if kind == "env":
                if it == "" or it[0].isdigit() or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_]*", it, re.ASCII):
                    return None
            else:
                if it == "" or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9._+/-]*", it, re.ASCII):
                    return None
        return items

    def read_conf(self, path: str) -> None:
        try:
            text = self.read_text(path)
        except OSError:
            return
        self.conf_state = "present"
        lines = text.split("\n")
        if lines and lines[-1] == "":
            lines.pop()
        for raw in lines:
            line = c_lstrip(raw)
            if line == "" or line.startswith("#"):
                continue
            if "=" not in line:
                self.diag("conf rejects a line without a key: %s" % line)
                continue
            key, val = line.split("=", 1)
            if key == "stack":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val in ("go", "npm", "python", "rust", "none"):
                    self.conf_stack.append(val)
                else:
                    self.diag("conf rejects stack '%s' (expected go, npm, python, rust or none)" % val)
            elif key in ("fast_gate", "full_command"):
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok(key, val, line):
                    continue
                if key == "fast_gate":
                    self.anon_fast += 1
                    name = "fast-%d" % self.anon_fast
                    self.conf_fast.append((name, val))
                else:
                    self.anon_full += 1
                    name = "full-%d" % self.anon_full
                    self.conf_full.append((name, val))
                self.conf_names.append(name)
            elif key == "check":
                if val.count("|") < 2:
                    self.diag("conf rejects check without the <name>|<fast|full>|<command> shape: %s" % line)
                    continue
                cname, rest = val.split("|", 1)
                cphase, ccmd = rest.split("|", 1)
                if cname == "" or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*", cname, re.ASCII):
                    self.diag("conf rejects check name '%s' (letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-',"
                             " starting with a letter or digit): %s" % (cname, line))
                    continue
                if re.fullmatch(r"(fast|full|scoped|micro)-[0-9].*", cname, re.ASCII):
                    self.diag("conf rejects check name '%s' (reserved for the anonymous and analyzer commands): %s" % (cname, line))
                    continue
                if cname == "audit" or cname.startswith("audit-"):
                    self.diag("conf rejects check name '%s' (reserved for the engine's dependency-audit gate): %s" % (cname, line))
                    continue
                if cphase not in ("fast", "full"):
                    self.diag("conf rejects check phase '%s' (expected fast or full): %s" % (cphase, line))
                    continue
                if cname in self.conf_names:
                    self.diag("conf rejects a second check named '%s': %s" % (cname, line))
                    continue
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok("check", ccmd, line):
                    continue
                self.conf_names.append(cname)
                (self.conf_fast if cphase == "fast" else self.conf_full).append((cname, ccmd))
            elif key in ("check_requires_env", "check_requires_cmd"):
                if "|" not in val:
                    self.diag("conf rejects %s without the <name>|item[,item] shape: %s" % (key, line))
                    continue
                rname, items_val = val.split("|", 1)
                kind = "env" if key == "check_requires_env" else "cmd"
                dest = self.conf_req_env if kind == "env" else self.conf_req_cmd
                items = self.conf_items(kind, items_val)
                if rname == "" or items is None:
                    self.diag("conf rejects %s value (expected <name>|item[,item] with valid %s items): %s" % (key, kind, line))
                    continue
                for it in items:
                    dest.append((rname, it))
            elif key == "check_timeout":
                if "|" not in val:
                    self.diag("conf rejects check_timeout without the <name>|<duration> shape: %s" % line)
                    continue
                rname, dur = val.split("|", 1)
                if (rname == "" or DUR_RE.fullmatch(dur) is None
                        or not re.search(r"[1-9]", dur)):
                    self.diag("conf rejects check_timeout value (expected <name>|<positive Go duration such as 20m or 1h30m>): %s" % line)
                    continue
                if any(n == rname for n, _ in self.conf_timeout):
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second check_timeout for '%s': %s" % (rname, line))
                    continue
                self.conf_timeout.append((rname, dur))
            elif key == "audit_prod_level":
                # polyforge#113: the one key that reaches the audit gate, and
                # it only tightens — a value below the default high threshold.
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects an empty audit_prod_level: %s" % line)
                elif val not in ("moderate", "low"):
                    self.diag("conf rejects audit_prod_level '%s' (expected moderate or low —"
                             " the key only tightens the audit gate's npm production threshold"
                             " below the default high): %s" % (val, line))
                elif self.conf_audit_prod_level != "":
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second audit_prod_level: %s" % line)
                else:
                    self.conf_audit_prod_level = val
            elif key == "host_requires":
                if val.count("|") < 2:
                    self.diag("conf rejects host_requires without the <name>|<probe>|<hint> shape: %s" % line)
                    continue
                hname, rest = val.split("|", 1)
                hprobe, hhint = rest.split("|", 1)
                if hname == "" or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*", hname, re.ASCII):
                    self.diag("conf rejects host_requires name '%s' (letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-',"
                             " starting with a letter or digit): %s" % (hname, line))
                    continue
                if c_rstrip(hprobe) == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects host_requires for '%s' with an empty probe: %s" % (hname, line))
                    continue
                if c_rstrip(hhint) == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects host_requires for '%s' with an empty install hint: %s" % (hname, line))
                    continue
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok("host_requires", hprobe, line):
                    continue
                if any(n == hname for n, _, _ in self.conf_host):
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second host_requires for '%s': %s" % (hname, line))
                    continue
                self.conf_host.append((hname, hprobe, hhint))
            elif key == "report":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val.count("|") < 2:
                    self.diag("conf rejects report without the <name>|<format>|<path> shape: %s" % line)
                    continue
                rname, rest = val.split("|", 1)
                rformat, rpath = rest.split("|", 1)
                if rname == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects report without a command name: %s" % line)
                    continue
                if rformat != "junit":
                    self.diag("conf rejects report format '%s' (expected junit): %s" % (rformat, line))
                    continue
                if (rpath == "" or rpath.startswith("/") or rpath == ".." or rpath.startswith("../")
                        or rpath.endswith("/..") or "/../" in rpath):
                    self.diag("conf rejects report path '%s' (expected one repository-relative path or glob,"
                             " not absolute, blank or ..-escaping): %s" % (rpath, line))
                    continue
                if any(n == rname for n, _, _ in self.conf_report):
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second report for '%s': %s" % (rname, line))
                    continue
                self.conf_report.append((rname, rformat, rpath))
            elif key == "rerun":
                if "|" not in val:
                    self.diag("conf rejects rerun without the <name>|<template> shape: %s" % line)
                    continue
                rname, rtmpl = val.split("|", 1)
                if rname == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects rerun without a command name: %s" % line)
                    continue
                if "{unit}" not in rtmpl:
                    self.diag("conf rejects rerun for '%s' without the {unit} placeholder: %s" % (rname, line))
                    continue
                if self.rerun_unit_quoted(rtmpl):
                    self.diag("conf rejects rerun for '%s' with {unit} inside quotes (the unit name is"
                             " shell-quoted where {unit} stands; leave it unquoted): %s" % (rname, line))
                    continue
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok("rerun", rtmpl, line):
                    continue
                if any(n == rname for n, _ in self.conf_rerun):
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second rerun for '%s': %s" % (rname, line))
                    continue
                self.conf_rerun.append((rname, rtmpl))
            elif key == "test_glob":
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf ignores an empty test_glob")
                    continue
                self.conf_globs.append(val)
            elif key == "base_setup":
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok(key, val, line):
                    continue
                self.conf_base_setup.append(val)
            elif key == "runner":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "complete":
                    self.conf_runner = "complete"
                else:
                    self.diag("conf rejects runner '%s' (expected complete)" % val)
            elif key == "ci_evidence":
                self.diag("conf rejects the removed key ci_evidence (no step reads it under the workflow contract): delete the line: %s" % line)
            elif key == "forced_full":
                if val:
                    self.conf_forced.append(val)
            elif key == "module_root":
                if val:
                    self.conf_roots.append(val)
            elif key == "exclude":
                if val:
                    self.conf_excl.append(val)
            elif key == "e2e_runner":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects an empty e2e_runner; falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                elif val.startswith("$(") or val.startswith("`"):
                    self.diag("conf rejects an e2e_runner value that begins with a command substitution; falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                elif self.conf_e2e_runner != "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects a second e2e_runner; falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                else:
                    self.conf_e2e_runner = val
            elif key == "e2e_start_threshold":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "" or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", val, re.ASCII) or int(val) == 0:
                    self.diag("conf rejects e2e_start_threshold '%s' (expected a positive integer)" % val)
                elif self.conf_e2e_start_threshold != "":
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second e2e_start_threshold: %s" % line)
                else:
                    self.conf_e2e_start_threshold = val
            elif key == "e2e_start_prefix":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects an empty e2e_start_prefix")
                elif val.startswith("$(") or val.startswith("`"):
                    self.diag("conf lint: e2e_start_prefix value begins with a command substitution"
                              " (lint diagnostic only; command values run as reported command: lines): %s" % line)
                elif self.conf_e2e_start_prefix != "":
                    self.diag("conf ignores a second e2e_start_prefix: %s" % line)
                else:
                    self.conf_e2e_start_prefix = val
            elif key == "suite_glob":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects an empty suite_glob; falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                    continue
                self.conf_suite_glob.append(val)
            elif key == "e2e_map":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if val.count("=") != 1 or val.split("=", 1)[0] == "" or val.split("=", 1)[1] == "":
                    self.diag("conf rejects e2e_map value (expected <glob>=<suite>, split by exactly one '=');"
                             " falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                    continue
                self.conf_e2e_map.append(val)
            elif key == "smoke_suite":
                self.smoke_declared = True
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if (val == "" or any(ch in val for ch in C_SPACE) or val.startswith("/")
                        or val.startswith("./") or val.startswith("../") or "/../" in val):
                    self.diag("conf rejects smoke_suite value (expected one repository-relative suite path);"
                             " falling back to the full tier: %s" % line)
                    self.conf_closed = True
                    continue
                if val not in self.conf_smoke:
                    self.conf_smoke.append(val)
            elif key == "micro_path":
                val = c_rstrip(val)
                if (val == "" or val.startswith("/") or val.startswith("./") or val.startswith("../")
                        or "/../" in val or val == ".." or val.endswith("/..")):
                    self.diag("conf rejects micro_path value (expected one repository-relative path pattern,"
                             " not absolute, ./-prefixed, blank or ..-escaping): %s" % line)
                    continue
                if val not in self.conf_micro_path:
                    self.conf_micro_path.append(val)
            elif key == "micro_check":
                if not self.conf_cmd_ok(key, val, line):
                    continue
                self.anon_micro += 1
                name = "micro-%d" % self.anon_micro
                self.conf_names.append(name)
                self.conf_micro.append((name, val))
            elif key == "go_harness":
                if val == "":
                    self.diag("conf ignores an empty go_harness")
                    continue
                self.conf_harness.append(val)
            elif key == "not_runnable_rule":
                # facts.py's replay key: the engine reads none of it, so it
                # accepts the line without the unknown-key diagnostic — no
                # conf reader diagnoses a key another tool owns.
                continue
            elif key in ("component", "epic_components", "epic_units", "epic_files"):
                # components.py's keys (polyforge#89): the component
                # declarations and the epic-upgrade thresholds are the
                # tool's — the engine reads none of them, so it accepts
                # the lines without the unknown-key diagnostic, the same
                # reader split as not_runnable_rule above.
                continue
            else:
                self.diag("conf rejects unknown key '%s'" % key)

    def conf_bind(self, key: str, records: List[Tuple[str, ...]]) -> List[Tuple[str, ...]]:
        """Keep a check_* (or report=) key's declarations whose name is a
        declared conf command and diagnose (and drop) the rest; the check=
        line may follow the binding line in the file."""
        kept: List[Tuple[str, ...]] = []
        for rec in records:
            if rec[0] in self.conf_names:
                kept.append(rec)
            else:
                self.diag("conf ignores %s for '%s', which names no declared command"
                          " (a check= name or fast-<i>/full-<i>/micro-<i>)" % (key, rec[0]))
        return kept

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The diff: affected paths of base..HEAD (sorted, unique), relative to
    # the engine root. Deleted files keep their pre-image path; a rename
    # contributes both the old and the new path. git names them from the
    # work tree's top: at a nested engine root (root_prefix) a path under
    # the root loses that prefix and a path outside it becomes
    # `../<path>`.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def diff_paths(self, rev: str) -> Optional[List[str]]:
        c = self.proc(["git", "diff", "--name-status", "-M", rev + "..HEAD"], cwd=self.root)
        if c.returncode != 0:
            return None
        raw: List[str] = []
        for line in c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n"):
            if line == "":
                continue
            status, rest = line.split("\t", 1)
            if status.startswith("R") or status.startswith("C"):
                old, new = rest.split("\t", 1)
                raw.append(old)
                raw.append(new)
            else:
                raw.append(rest)
        if self.root_prefix != "" and raw:
            up = self.root_up()
            rel: List[str] = []
            for p in raw:
                if p.startswith(self.root_prefix):
                    rel.append(p[len(self.root_prefix):])
                else:
                    rel.append(up + p)
            raw = rel
        return sorted(set(raw)) if raw else []

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The Go analyzer.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def go_graph(self) -> bool:
        """Build the Go package graph once: self.packages (one
        `import|dir|closure` entry per package, the closure that of its
        test binary) and self.pkg_dirs. False when the graph cannot be
        had, the reason in go_graph_why. The graph is listed with
        `go list -e`, so a package error is data, not a failed listing:
        each one rides its record as a `!<error>` line (the package's own
        error and every dependency error). An embed pattern that matches
        no file is tolerated: it changes no package's dependencies, so the
        graph is the one the built tree gives. Any other package error
        fails the graph closed."""
        if self.go_graph_state == "ok":
            return True
        if self.go_graph_state == "failed":
            return False
        self.go_graph_state = "failed"
        fmt = ('{{.ImportPath}}|{{.Dir}}|{{join .Deps " "}}|{{len .TestGoFiles}}|{{len .XTestGoFiles}}'
               '{{with .Error}}{{"\\n"}}!{{.Err}}{{end}}{{range .DepsErrors}}{{"\\n"}}!{{.Err}}{{end}}')
        try:
            c = self.proc(["go", "list", "-e", "-test", "-f", fmt, "./..."],
                         cwd=self.root, env=self.child_env({"GOWORK": "off"}))
        except OSError:
            self.go_graph_why = ("go list failed: `GOWORK=off go list -e -test -f <record format> ./...`"
                                 " could not start")
            return False
        if c.returncode != 0:
            err_first = ""
            for ln in c.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n"):
                if ln.strip() != "":
                    err_first = cut300(ln)
                    break
            if err_first == "":
                err_first = "no output"
            self.go_graph_why = ("go list failed: `GOWORK=off go list -e -test -f <record format> ./...`"
                                  " exited %d: %s" % (c.returncode, err_first))
            return False
        out = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")
        for ln in out.split("\n"):
            if ln.startswith("!") and not re.fullmatch(r"!pattern [^ ]+: no matching files found", ln, re.ASCII):
                self.go_graph_why = ("go list failed: `GOWORK=off go list -e -test -f <record format> ./...`"
                                     " reports a package error: %s" % cut300(ln[1:]))
                return False
        records = [ln for ln in out.split("\n") if ln != "" and not ln.startswith("!")]
        # Fail closed on an unexpected go list -test shape. Every record
        # must carry the import path, the package directory, the dependency
        # closure and the two test-file counts; every package that has
        # test files must also have its `P.test` record, whose closure the
        # fold below needs.
        testrec: Set[str] = set()
        hastests: Set[str] = set()
        for ln in records:
            f = ln.split("|")
            if len(f) < 5:
                self.go_graph_why = "unexpected go list -test output shape"
                return False
            if f[0].endswith(".test"):
                testrec.add(f[1])
                continue
            if " [" in f[0]:
                continue
            if awk_num(f[3]) > 0 or awk_num(f[4]) > 0:
                hastests.add(f[1])
        if not hastests <= testrec:
            self.go_graph_why = "unexpected go list -test output shape"
            return False
        # go list -test also emits each package's test binary (`P.test`),
        # whose Deps is the closure of everything `go test` compiles for P
        # — test imports included. Fold that closure into P's plain entry,
        # so the reverse-dependency scan below selects a package reached
        # only through a `_test.go` import. The test-variant entries
        # (`P [P.test]`, `P_test [P.test]`) are dropped: `go test ./P`
        # already runs them with P.
        tdeps: Dict[str, str] = {}
        for ln in records:
            f = ln.split("|", 3)
            if f[0].endswith(".test"):
                tdeps[f[1]] = f[2]
        packages: List[Tuple[str, str, str]] = []
        for ln in records:
            f = ln.split("|", 3)
            if " [" in f[0] or f[0].endswith(".test"):
                continue
            packages.append((f[0], f[1], tdeps.get(f[1], f[2])))
        self.packages = packages
        self.pkg_dirs = {rel_dir(self.root, d) for _, d, _ in packages}
        self.go_graph_state = "ok"
        return True

    def go_map_dirs(self, paths: List[str]) -> List[str]:
        """Each path listed mapped to its nearest ancestor `go list` package
        directory (sorted, unique): a file in `<pkg>/testdata/` or another
        package subdirectory belongs to its owning package. A path with no
        ancestor package maps to nothing."""
        out: Set[str] = set()
        for p in paths:
            d = path_dir(p)
            while True:
                if d in self.pkg_dirs:
                    out.add(d)
                    break
                if d == ".":
                    break
                d = path_dir(d)
        return sorted(out)

    def go_closure(self, dirs: List[str], with_harness: bool = False) -> List[str]:
        """The directories of the packages mapped in dirs plus every package
        that (transitively, through its test binary) depends on one — the
        reverse-dependency closure. A declared harness owns its directory,
        which stays out (the scoped selection runs it through the harness
        instead) unless with_harness; otherwise it is an ordinary
        package."""
        want = set(dirs)
        closure_imports: Set[str] = set()
        for imp, d, _ in self.packages:
            if rel_dir(self.root, d) in want:
                closure_imports.add(imp)
        if not closure_imports:
            return []
        out: Set[str] = set()
        for imp, d, deps in self.packages:
            rel = rel_dir(self.root, d)
            if (self.go_harness != "" and rel == self.go_harness_dir and not with_harness):
                continue
            sel = rel in want
            if not sel:
                if set(deps.split()) & closure_imports:
                    sel = True
            if sel:
                out.add(rel)
        return sorted(out)

    def git_grep_literal(self, needle: str, patterns_file: Optional[str] = None) -> List[str]:
        """`git grep -F -l` output (one call per -e needle, or one -f file
        call), paths relative to the engine root."""
        if patterns_file is not None:
            argv = ["git", "grep", "-F", "-l", "-f", patterns_file]
        else:
            argv = ["git", "grep", "-F", "-l", "-e", needle]
        try:
            c = self.proc(argv, cwd=self.root)
        except OSError:
            return []
        return [ln for ln in c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n") if ln != ""]

    def go_test_names(self, f: str) -> List[str]:
        """The `func Test...` names a file declares (sorted, unique)."""
        try:
            text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
        except OSError:
            return []
        return sorted({m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r"^func (Test[^ (]*).*", text, re.ASCII | re.MULTILINE)})

    def add_scoped(self, cmd: str) -> None:
        self.scoped_cmds.append(cmd)

    def compute_scoped_go(self) -> bool:
        if not self.go_graph():
            self.diag("%s; falling back to the full tier" % self.go_graph_why)
            return False
        # Changed packages plus every package that (transitively) depends on
        # one.
        mapped_dirs = self.go_map_dirs(self.changed)
        selected = self.go_closure(mapped_dirs)
        if selected:
            pkgargs = ""
            for rel in selected:
                pkgargs += " ." if rel == "." else " ./" + rel
            self.add_scoped("GOWORK=off go test -count=1" + pkgargs)
        # Tests that textually name a changed path, per package not already
        # in the package command. A declared harness is handled below
        # instead.
        tr_pairs: Set[Tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for p in self.changed:
            for f in self.git_grep_literal(p):
                if f == "":
                    continue
                if self.go_harness != "" and f.startswith(self.go_harness_dir + "/"):
                    continue
                if not f.endswith("_test.go"):
                    continue
                names = self.go_test_names(f)
                if not names:
                    continue
                d = path_dir(f)
                for name in names:
                    tr_pairs.add((d, name))
        if tr_pairs:
            for d in sorted({d for d, _ in tr_pairs}):
                if d in selected:
                    continue
                names = sorted({n for dd, n in tr_pairs if dd == d})
                pkg = "." if d == "." else "./" + d
                self.add_scoped("GOWORK=off go test -count=1 -run '^(%s)$' %s" % (join_pipe(names), pkg))
        # The repo's declared harness runs the acceptance tests whose
        # source names a changed path, by test name.
        if self.go_harness != "":
            e2e_names: Set[str] = set()
            for p in self.changed:
                for f in self.git_grep_literal(p):
                    if f == "":
                        continue
                    if self.go_harness == "" or not f.startswith(self.go_harness_dir + "/") or not f.endswith(".go"):
                        continue
                    e2e_names.update(self.go_test_names(f))
            if e2e_names:
                names = sorted(e2e_names)
                self.add_scoped("E2E_RUN='^(%s)$' sh %s" % (join_pipe(names), self.go_harness))
        return True

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Carries layer (stack-neutral). A suite declares the repository paths
    # it covers, relative to the engine root, in its opening comment block
    # (the leading blank and comment (`//` or `#`) lines before the first
    # line of code); several carries: lines union; globs are separated by
    # commas or blanks and use the shell-case syntax forced_full= and
    # exclude= use (`*` crosses `/`).
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def cr_list_files(self) -> None:
        """The repository's files (tracked and untracked, ignored files left
        out), repo-relative and sorted."""
        if self.inside_work_tree():
            c = self.proc(["git", "-c", "core.quotePath=off", "ls-files", "-co", "--exclude-standard"],
                         cwd=self.root)
            listed = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n")
        else:
            listed = []
            for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(self.root):
                dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d != ".git"]
                for fn in filenames:
                    listed.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, fn), self.root))
        self.cr_files = [f for f in sorted(set(ln for ln in listed if ln != ""))
                         if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, f))]

    def cr_default_discover(self) -> List[str]:
        out: List[str] = []
        if self.analyzer == "npm":
            for f in self.cr_files:
                if re.fullmatch(r"e2e/[^/]*\.mjs", f, re.ASCII):
                    out.append(f)
        elif self.analyzer == "python":
            # pytest collection: test_*.py / *_test.py under e2e/ or
            # tests/e2e/; conftest.py, __init__.py and other modules there
            # are not suites.
            for f in self.cr_files:
                if re.match(r"(tests/)?e2e/", f) and re.search(r"(^|/)(test_[^/]*|[^/]*_test)\.py$", f):
                    out.append(f)
        elif self.analyzer == "rust":
            # Integration-test crates: top-level tests/*.rs only (tests/common/
            # and other subdirectories hold helper modules).
            for f in self.cr_files:
                if re.fullmatch(r"tests/[^/]*\.rs", f, re.ASCII):
                    out.append(f)
        return out

    def cr_is_module(self, f: str) -> bool:
        a = self.analyzer
        if a == "npm":
            return f.endswith((".mjs", ".js", ".cjs", ".ts", ".tsx", ".mts", ".cts", ".jsx"))
        if a == "python":
            return f.endswith(".py")
        if a == "rust":
            return f.endswith(".rs")
        return False

    def cr_norm(self, d: str, spec: str) -> str:
        """A repo-relative directory and a relative specifier joined,
        folding `.` and `..`; "" when the result leaves the repository."""
        stack: List[str] = []
        for part in (d + "/" + spec).split("/"):
            if part == "" or part == ".":
                continue
            if part == "..":
                if not stack:
                    return ""
                stack.pop()
                continue
            stack.append(part)
        return "/".join(stack)

    def cr_imports(self, f: str) -> List[Tuple[str, str]]:
        """(importer, target) for every relative import of a module, or
        (importer, "?" + specifier) for one that does not resolve."""
        d = path_dir(f)
        out: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        if self.analyzer == "npm":
            try:
                text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
            except OSError:
                text = ""
            specs = sorted(set(m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(
                r"(?:from|import|require)[ \t\r\f\v]*\(?[ \t\r\f\v]*['\"](\.\.?/[^'\"\n]+)['\"]", text)))
            for spec in specs:
                if spec == "":
                    continue
                t = self.cr_norm(d, spec)
                hit = ""
                if t != "":
                    for cand in (t, t + ".mjs", t + ".js", t + ".cjs", t + ".ts", t + ".tsx", t + ".mts",
                                 t + ".jsx", t + "/index.mjs", t + "/index.js", t + "/index.ts", t + "/index.tsx"):
                        if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, cand)):
                            hit = cand
                            break
                out.append((f, hit) if hit != "" else (f, "?" + spec))
        elif self.analyzer == "python":
            out = [(imp, tgt) for imp, tgt in self.py_edges if imp == f]
        elif self.analyzer == "rust":
            out = [(imp, tgt) for imp, tgt in self.rs_edges if imp == f]
        return out

    def cr_runner_targets(self) -> None:
        """The files the declared e2e_runner= runs (through the npm script
        when the value is `npm run <script> ...`): runners, not suites."""
        self.cr_runner = []
        if self.conf_e2e_runner == "":
            return
        words = self.conf_e2e_runner.split()
        if len(words) >= 3 and words[0] == "npm" and words[1] == "run":
            for name, cmd in self.npm_scripts:
                if name == words[2]:
                    words = words + cmd.split()
                    break
        for w in words:
            w = w[2:] if w.startswith("./") else w
            if w != "" and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, w)):
                self.cr_runner.append(w)
        self.cr_runner = sorted(set(self.cr_runner))

    def carries_discover(self) -> None:
        """Enumerate the candidates, follow their imports, parse every
        declaration and answer the suite set in cr_suites. It writes no
        diagnostic: the scoped computation reports what it finds."""
        self.cr_runner_targets()
        if self.conf_suite_glob:
            matched: List[str] = []
            for f in self.cr_files:
                for g in self.conf_suite_glob:
                    if case_match(g, f):
                        matched.append(f)
                        break
        else:
            matched = self.cr_default_discover()
        self.cr_cand = sorted({f for f in matched
                               if not self.path_excluded(f) and f not in set(self.cr_runner)})
        # The import graph: every module reachable from a candidate.
        self.cr_nodes = set(self.cr_cand)
        frontier = list(self.cr_cand)
        while frontier:
            nxt: List[str] = []
            for f in frontier:
                if not self.cr_is_module(f):
                    continue
                for imp, tgt in self.cr_imports(f):
                    if tgt.startswith("?"):
                        self.cr_unres.append((imp, tgt[1:]))
                    else:
                        self.cr_edges.append((imp, tgt))
                        nxt.append(tgt)
            new = sorted(set(nxt) - self.cr_nodes)
            frontier = new
            self.cr_nodes.update(new)
        imported = {tgt for _, tgt in self.cr_edges}
        # Declarations: the header, or the conf's e2e_map= lines, which
        # replace it.
        map_targets = {m.split("=", 1)[1] for m in self.conf_e2e_map}
        for f in self.cr_cand:
            if f in map_targets:
                self.cr_status.append((f, "declared"))
                for m in self.conf_e2e_map:
                    if m.split("=", 1)[1] == f:
                        self.cr_decl.append((f, m.split("=", 1)[0]))
                continue
            try:
                src = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
            except OSError:
                src = ""
            status, globs = parse_header(src)
            self.cr_status.append((f, status))
            for g in globs:
                self.cr_decl.append((f, g))
        cand_set = set(self.cr_cand)
        for m in self.conf_e2e_map:
            if m.split("=", 1)[1] not in cand_set:
                self.cr_maperr.append(m.split("=", 1)[1])
        # Suites: every candidate except an undeclared one another module
        # imports.
        self.cr_suites = sorted({f for f, st in self.cr_status
                                if not (st == "undeclared" and f in imported)})

    def cr_importer_suites(self, module: str) -> List[str]:
        """Every suite that (transitively) imports a module."""
        front = {module}
        seen = {module}
        while front:
            importers = {imp for imp, tgt in self.cr_edges if tgt in front}
            new = importers - seen
            seen |= new
            front = new
        return [s for s in self.cr_suites if s in seen and s != module]

    def cr_suite_cmd(self, suite: str) -> str:
        if self.analyzer == "npm":
            return "node %s" % suite
        if self.analyzer == "python":
            return "pytest %s" % suite
        return "cargo test --test %s" % suite.rsplit("/", 1)[-1][:-len(".rs")]

    def cr_structure(self) -> bool:
        """Diagnose every structural reason the declarations cannot be
        trusted — an undeclared or malformed suite, an unresolvable import
        — answering whether there is one (the e2e_map= check is the
        caller's, it holds even without a suite)."""
        bad = False
        suites = set(self.cr_suites)
        for f, st in self.cr_status:
            if f not in suites:
                continue
            if st == "undeclared":
                self.diag("carries: suite %s declares no carries: header in its opening comment block;"
                         " falling back to the full tier" % f)
                bad = True
            elif st == "malformed":
                self.diag("carries: suite %s has a malformed carries: declaration (an empty value or a"
                          " carries line without its colon); falling back to the full tier" % f)
                bad = True
        for imp, spec in self.cr_unres:
            self.diag("carries: %s imports %s, which does not resolve; the import graph is unresolvable,"
                     " falling back to the full tier" % (imp, spec))
            bad = True
        return bad

    def cr_maperr_diag(self) -> bool:
        bad = False
        for s in self.cr_maperr:
            self.diag("carries: e2e_map names %s, which is not a discovered suite; falling back to the full tier" % s)
            bad = True
        return bad

    def cr_select(self, paths: List[str]) -> Tuple[List[str], List[str]]:
        """The suites the paths select (sorted, unique), and every such path
        that is neither documentation, an exclude= match, a suite, a helper
        nor covered by a declaration."""
        selected: Set[str] = set()
        uncov: List[str] = []
        suites = set(self.cr_suites)
        for p in paths:
            if p == "":
                continue
            hit = False
            if p in suites:
                selected.add(p)
                hit = True
            if p in self.cr_nodes:
                imps = self.cr_importer_suites(p)
                if imps:
                    selected.update(imps)
                    hit = True
            for s, g in self.cr_decl:
                if case_match(g, p):
                    selected.add(s)
                    hit = True
            if hit:
                continue
            if self.is_doc_path(p) or self.path_excluded(p):
                continue
            uncov.append(p)
        return (sorted(s for s in selected if s in suites), uncov)

    def cr_suite_cmds(self, suites: List[str]) -> List[str]:
        """The command(s) that run the suites: one e2e_runner= command,
        else the analyzer's default command per suite."""
        if not suites:
            return []
        prefix = ""
        if (self.light and self.conf_e2e_start_threshold != ""
                and len(suites) > int(self.conf_e2e_start_threshold)
                and self.conf_e2e_start_prefix != ""):
            prefix = self.conf_e2e_start_prefix + " "
        if self.conf_e2e_runner != "":
            return ["%s%s %s" % (prefix, self.conf_e2e_runner, " ".join(suites))]
        return [self.cr_suite_cmd(s) for s in suites]

    def carries_scoped(self) -> bool:
        """Add the selected suites to the scoped commands; answer whether
        the declarations account for the diff. The scoped tier never adds
        the smoke suites and an uncovered path still falls back to the
        full tier."""
        bad = self.cr_maperr_diag()
        if not self.cr_suites:
            return not bad
        if self.cr_structure():
            bad = True
        sel, uncov = self.cr_select(self.changed)
        for p in uncov:
            if self.light:
                self.diag("--light: carries: changed path %s is covered by no carries: declaration;"
                         " no suite is selected for it (the fast gates cover it)" % p)
                continue
            self.diag("carries: changed path %s is covered by no carries: declaration;"
                     " falling back to the full tier" % p)
            bad = True
        if bad:
            return False
        for c in self.cr_suite_cmds(sel):
            self.add_scoped(c)
        return True

    def compute_scoped_npm(self) -> bool:
        return self.carries_scoped()

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Python analyzer. Imports are scanned statically (`import a.b`,
    # `from a import b`, relative `from .x import y`) and resolved to
    # repository modules (from the root, from src/, or beside the
    # importer, as pytest's rootdir insertion does); an absolute import
    # that names no repository module is a third-party import, while a
    # relative one that resolves nowhere is an unresolvable import.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    def py_scan(self) -> None:
        exists = set(self.cr_files)
        edges: Set[Tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for f in self.cr_files:
            if not f.endswith(".py"):
                continue
            d = f.rsplit("/", 1)[0] if "/" in f else ""
            try:
                text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
            except OSError:
                continue

            def res(p: str) -> str:
                if p + ".py" in exists:
                    return p + ".py"
                if p + "/__init__.py" in exists:
                    return p + "/__init__.py"
                return ""

            def emit(r: str) -> None:
                if r != "" and r != f:
                    edges.add((f, r))

            def absimp(m: str) -> None:
                p = m.replace(".", "/")
                r = res(p)
                if r == "":
                    r = res("src/" + p)
                if r == "" and d != "":
                    r = res(d + "/" + p)
                emit(r)

            for raw in text.split("\n"):
                line = raw.split("#", 1)[0]
                m_imp = re.match(r"[ \t]*import[ \t]+", line)
                if m_imp:
                    rest = line[m_imp.end():]
                    for part in rest.split(","):
                        mword = re.split(r"[ \t]", part.lstrip(" \t"), 1)[0]
                        if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.]*", mword, re.ASCII):
                            absimp(mword)
                    continue
                m_from = re.match(r"[ \t]*from[ \t]+[.A-Za-z0-9_]+[ \t]+import[ \t]+", line)
                if not m_from:
                    continue
                hdr = m_from.group(0)
                rest = line[m_from.end():]
                base = re.sub(r"^[ \t]*from[ \t]+", "", hdr)
                base = re.sub(r"[ \t]+import[ \t]+$", "", base)
                rest = re.sub(r"[()\\]", " ", rest)
                names = [re.split(r"[ \t]", p.lstrip(" \t"), 1)[0] for p in rest.split(",")]
                if not base.startswith("."):
                    absimp(base)
                    for nm in names:
                        if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", nm, re.ASCII):
                            absimp(base + "." + nm)
                    continue
                dots = len(base) - len(base.lstrip("."))
                mod = base[dots:]
                pdir = d
                unres = False
                for _ in range(1, dots):
                    if pdir == "":
                        edges.add((f, "?" + base))
                        unres = True
                        break
                    if "/" in pdir:
                        pdir = pdir.rsplit("/", 1)[0]
                    else:
                        pdir = ""
                if unres:
                    continue
                prefix = (pdir + "/") if pdir != "" else ""
                modpath = mod.replace(".", "/")
                if modpath != "":
                    r = res(prefix + modpath)
                    if r == "":
                        edges.add((f, "?" + base))
                        continue
                    emit(r)
                    for nm in names:
                        if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", nm, re.ASCII):
                            emit(res(prefix + modpath + "/" + nm))
                    continue
                hit = False
                for nm in names:
                    if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", nm, re.ASCII):
                        continue
                    r = res(prefix + nm)
                    if r != "":
                        emit(r)
                        hit = True
                r = res(prefix + "__init__")
                if r != "":
                    emit(r)
                    hit = True
                if not hit:
                    edges.add((f, "?" + base))
        self.py_edges = sorted(edges)

    @staticmethod
    def is_py_test(f: str) -> bool:
        base = f.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        return (base.startswith("test_") and base.endswith(".py")) or base.endswith("_test.py")

    def rev_closure(self, edges: Sequence[Tuple[str, str]], start: str) -> List[str]:
        """Every node that (transitively) reaches the start node, the start
        excluded."""
        front = {start}
        seen = {start}
        while front:
            reach = {imp for imp, tgt in edges if tgt in front}
            new = reach - seen
            seen |= new
            front = new
        return sorted(seen - {start})

    def compute_scoped_python(self) -> bool:
        """Select the unit tests (pytest-collected files outside the suite
        set) that import a changed module, directly or through other
        repository modules, then the carries suites. A changed module no
        test reaches is a low-confidence scope: the full tier."""
        py_bad = False
        py_units: Set[str] = set()
        cand = set(self.cr_cand)
        for p in self.changed:
            if not p.endswith(".py"):
                continue
            reach = [p] + self.rev_closure(self.py_edges, p)
            found = False
            for r in reach:
                if not self.is_py_test(r):
                    continue
                found = True
                if r in cand:
                    continue
                if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, r)):
                    py_units.add(r)
            if not found:
                self.diag("python: no test imports the changed module %s (directly or through another module);"
                         " its affected tests cannot be resolved, falling back to the full tier" % p)
                py_bad = True
        if py_units:
            self.add_scoped("pytest %s" % " ".join(sorted(py_units)))
        if not self.carries_scoped():
            py_bad = True
        return not py_bad

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Rust analyzer. The module tree is scanned from `mod <name>;`
    # declarations (a crate root — src/lib.rs, src/main.rs, a mod.rs or a
    # top-level tests/*.rs — keeps its children beside it, any other
    # module in its own directory); a declaration that names no file is
    # unresolvable. A changed module selects the unit tests of the modules
    # that contain it or `use` it (`crate::<path>`), filtered by module
    # path, plus the carries suites.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    def rs_scan(self) -> None:
        exists = set(self.cr_files)
        edges: Set[Tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for f in self.cr_files:
            if not f.endswith(".rs"):
                continue
            d = f.rsplit("/", 1)[0] if "/" in f else ""
            b = f.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
            if b in ("lib.rs", "main.rs", "mod.rs") or re.fullmatch(r"tests/[^/]*\.rs", f, re.ASCII):
                cdir = d
            else:
                cdir = f[:-len(".rs")]
            prefix = (cdir + "/") if cdir != "" else ""
            try:
                text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
            except OSError:
                continue
            for m in re.finditer(r"^[ \t]*(pub(\([^)]*\))?[ \t]+)?mod[ \t]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*;",
                                  text, re.ASCII | re.MULTILINE):
                name = re.sub(r"^[ \t]*(pub(\([^)]*\))?[ \t]+)?mod[ \t]+", "", m.group(0))
                name = re.sub(r"[ \t]*;.*$", "", name)
                if prefix + name + ".rs" in exists:
                    edges.add((f, prefix + name + ".rs"))
                elif prefix + name + "/mod.rs" in exists:
                    edges.add((f, prefix + name + "/mod.rs"))
                else:
                    edges.add((f, "?mod " + name))
        self.rs_edges = sorted(edges)

    def fwd_closure(self, start: str) -> List[str]:
        """Every node the rust module graph reaches from the start node, the
        start included."""
        front = {start}
        seen = {start}
        while front:
            reach = {tgt for imp, tgt in self.rs_edges if imp in front and not tgt.startswith("?")}
            new = reach - seen
            seen |= new
            front = new
        return sorted(seen)

    @staticmethod
    def rs_modpath(f: str) -> str:
        m = f[len("src/"):] if f.startswith("src/") else f
        if m.endswith(".rs"):
            m = m[:-len(".rs")]
        if m.endswith("/mod"):
            m = m[:-len("/mod")]
        return m.replace("/", "::")

    def compute_scoped_rust(self) -> bool:
        rs_bad = False
        crate = ""
        try:
            cargo = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, "Cargo.toml"))
        except OSError:
            cargo = ""
        in_pkg = False
        for ln in cargo.split("\n"):
            if ln.strip().startswith("["):
                in_pkg = ln.strip() == "[package]"
                continue
            if in_pkg:
                m = re.match(r"[ \t]*name[ \t]*=[ \t]*\"([^\"]*)\"", ln)
                if m:
                    crate = m.group(1).replace("-", "_")
                    break
        rs_lib: List[str] = []
        rs_bin: List[str] = []
        if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "src/lib.rs")):
            rs_lib = self.fwd_closure("src/lib.rs")
        if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "src/main.rs")):
            rs_bin = self.fwd_closure("src/main.rs")
        rs_lib_filters: Set[str] = set()
        rs_bin_filters: Set[str] = set()
        tests_files = [f for f in self.cr_files if re.fullmatch(r"tests/.*\.rs", f, re.ASCII)]
        for p in self.changed:
            if not p.endswith(".rs"):
                continue
            if p.startswith("tests/"):
                continue
            if p in ("src/lib.rs", "src/main.rs"):
                self.diag("rust: the crate root %s changed; its affected tests cannot be narrowed,"
                         " falling back to the full tier" % p)
                rs_bad = True
                continue
            if p in set(rs_lib):
                kind, kind_files = "lib", rs_lib
            elif p in set(rs_bin):
                kind, kind_files = "bin", rs_bin
            else:
                self.diag("rust: the changed file %s is in no crate's module tree (no mod declaration reaches it);"
                         " its affected tests cannot be resolved, falling back to the full tier" % p)
                rs_bad = True
                continue
            # The changed module and every module of the same crate that
            # uses it, transitively.
            rs_set = {p}
            front = {p}
            while front:
                nxt: Set[str] = set()
                for q in front:
                    mp = self.rs_modpath(q)
                    pat = re.compile("crate::" + re.escape(mp) + r"([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)", re.ASCII | re.MULTILINE)
                    for cand in kind_files:
                        try:
                            text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, cand))
                        except OSError:
                            continue
                        if pat.search(text):
                            nxt.add(cand)
                new = nxt - rs_set
                rs_set |= new
                front = new
            found = False
            for q in rs_set:
                mp = self.rs_modpath(q)
                try:
                    text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, q))
                except OSError:
                    text = ""
                if re.search(r"#\[(test|cfg\(test\))\]", text):
                    (rs_lib_filters if kind == "lib" else rs_bin_filters).add(mp + "::")
                    found = True
                if crate != "":
                    ipat = re.compile(re.escape(crate) + "::" + re.escape(mp) + r"([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)",
                                       re.ASCII | re.MULTILINE)
                    for cand in tests_files:
                        try:
                            ctext = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, cand))
                        except OSError:
                            continue
                        if ipat.search(ctext):
                            found = True
                            break
            if not found:
                self.diag("rust: no unit test module or integration test reaches the changed module %s;"
                         " its affected tests cannot be resolved, falling back to the full tier" % p)
                rs_bad = True
        if rs_lib_filters:
            self.add_scoped("cargo test --lib -- %s" % " ".join(sorted(rs_lib_filters)))
        if rs_bin_filters:
            self.add_scoped("cargo test --bins -- %s" % " ".join(sorted(rs_bin_filters)))
        if not self.carries_scoped():
            rs_bad = True
        return not rs_bad

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Full-tier e2e selection. A repository opts in by declaring at least
    # one smoke_suite=; the caller asked for the full tier (--full) and
    # the diff is non-empty.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    def sel_fallback(self, why: str) -> None:
        self.diag("full-tier e2e selection falls back to the full e2e command: %s" % why)
        self.sel_mode = "full"

    def full_tier_selection(self) -> None:
        self.sel_mode = ""
        if self.conf_closed:
            self.sel_fallback("a carries or smoke_suite key of the conf is empty or malformed")
        if self.analyzer not in ("npm", "python", "rust"):
            self.sel_fallback("the %s analyzer has no carries layer" % (self.analyzer or "absent"))
        for p in self.changed:
            if p == "":
                continue
            if self.path_forced(p):
                self.sel_fallback("changed path %s is forced full" % p)
        for p in self.outside:
            self.sel_fallback("changed path %s is outside the engine root" % p)
        if self.conf_changed == "true":
            self.sel_fallback("the diff changes .polyforge/verify-scope.conf")
        if self.analyzer in ("npm", "python", "rust"):
            if self.cr_maperr_diag():
                self.sel_fallback("an e2e_map= names no discovered suite")
            if self.cr_structure():
                self.sel_fallback("the carries declarations are structurally broken")
        for s in self.conf_smoke:
            if s not in set(self.cr_suites):
                self.sel_fallback("smoke_suite %s is not a discovered suite" % s)
        if not self.sel_full:
            self.sel_fallback("the full tail is empty")
        if self.sel_mode == "full":
            return
        sel, uncov = self.cr_select(self.changed)
        self.sel_mode = "smoke"
        for p in self.changed:
            if p == "":
                continue
            if self.is_doc_path(p) or self.path_excluded(p):
                continue
            self.sel_mode = "carries"
            break
        if self.sel_mode == "carries":
            self.sel_suites = sorted(set(sel) | set(self.conf_smoke))
            self.sel_uncov = uncov
        else:
            self.sel_suites = sorted(set(self.conf_smoke))

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Dependency-audit gate: the one `audit` command for the repository's
    # auditable surfaces — the root go.mod (--go) and every
    # package-lock.json/npm-shrinkwrap.json outside node_modules/
    # (--npm <dir>, sorted) — run by the audit-deps helper shipped beside
    # this engine; "" when the repository has no surface. exclude= does
    # not apply. The ledger is the repository's one, at the top of the
    # git work tree. A declared audit_prod_level (polyforge#113, the one
    # conf key that reaches the gate, tightening only) appends
    # --npm-prod-level after the surfaces, before the ledger.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def audit_command(self) -> str:
        surfaces = ""
        if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "go.mod")):
            surfaces = " --go"
        npm_dirs: Set[str] = set()
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(self.root):
            dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d not in ("node_modules", ".git")]
            for fn in filenames:
                if fn in ("package-lock.json", "npm-shrinkwrap.json"):
                    npm_dirs.add(rel_dir(self.root, dirpath))
        for d in sorted(npm_dirs):
            if d != "":
                surfaces += " --npm " + sq(d)
        if surfaces == "":
            return ""
        ledger = ""
        if self.root_prefix != "":
            ledger = " --ledger " + sq(self.root_up() + ".polyforge/audit-exemptions.conf")
        level = ""
        if self.conf_audit_prod_level != "":
            level = " --npm-prod-level " + self.conf_audit_prod_level
        return "sh %s%s%s%s" % (sq(os.path.join(self.engine_dir, "audit-deps")), surfaces, level, ledger)

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # npm helpers.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def npm_has_script(self, name: str) -> bool:
        return any(n == name for n, _ in self.npm_scripts)

    def npm_has_typescript(self) -> bool:
        if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "tsconfig.json")):
            return True
        try:
            pkg = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, "package.json"))
        except OSError:
            return False
        return re.search(r'"typescript"[ \t\r\f\v]*:', pkg) is not None

    def npm_script_suites(self) -> List[Tuple[str, str]]:
        """(script name, e2e/file) for every declared script whose command
        runs an e2e/*.mjs suite, so the suite is reported through its
        script and not repeated standalone."""
        out: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []
        for name, cmd in self.npm_scripts:
            if name == "":
                continue
            refs = sorted(set(tok for tok in re.split(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._/-]+", cmd)
                              if re.fullmatch(r"e2e/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\.mjs", tok, re.ASCII)))
            for ref in refs:
                out.append((name, ref))
        return out

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # --since <rev>.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    def since_textrefs(self, paths: List[str]) -> Tuple[List[str], bool]:
        """The directories of the non-harness packages whose *_test.go
        declares a test and textually names a path listed in paths, and
        whether a test of the declared harness does."""
        dirs: Set[str] = set()
        harness_hit = False
        if not paths:
            return [], False
        pfile = os.path.join(self.tmp, "since_patterns")
        with open(pfile, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("\n".join(paths) + "\n")
        for f in self.git_grep_literal("", patterns_file=pfile):
            if f == "" or not f.endswith(".go"):
                continue
            try:
                text = self.read_text(os.path.join(self.root, f))
            except OSError:
                continue
            if not re.search(r"^func Test", text, re.MULTILINE):
                continue
            if self.go_harness != "" and f.startswith(self.go_harness_dir + "/"):
                harness_hit = True
                continue
            if f.endswith("_test.go"):
                dirs.add(path_dir(f))
        return sorted(dirs), harness_hit

    def since_side_all(self, paths: List[str]) -> Tuple[Optional[str], str]:
        """(the trigger path, why) when the paths make that side's affected
        set the whole repository. A path in the declared go_harness's
        directory is one: the harness's tests drive the whole built
        program, whatever they import."""
        for p in paths:
            if p == "":
                continue
            if p.startswith("../"):
                return p, "%s is outside the engine root" % p
            if self.go_harness != "" and p.startswith(self.go_harness_dir + "/"):
                return p, ("%s is in the declared go_harness directory %s, whose tests drive the whole program"
                           % (p, self.go_harness_dir))
            if self.path_forced(p):
                return p, "%s is forced full" % p
        return None, ""

    def since_query(self) -> None:
        own = self.changed
        self.since_hits = []
        self.since_intersects = "true"
        self.since_reason = ""
        self.since_head = self.since_arg
        sha = ""
        if self.inside_work_tree():
            c = self.proc(["git", "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", self.since_arg + "^{commit}"], cwd=self.root)
            if c.returncode == 0:
                sha = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
        if sha == "":
            self.since_reason = "cannot resolve --since %s to a commit; failing closed" % self.since_arg
            return
        self.since_head = sha
        short = sha[:12]
        if not self.diff_ok:
            self.since_reason = "the work item's own diff (base..HEAD) cannot be read; failing closed"
            return
        upstream = self.diff_paths(sha)
        if upstream is None:
            self.since_reason = "cannot diff %s..HEAD; failing closed" % short
            return
        if not upstream:
            self.since_intersects = "false"
            self.since_reason = "nothing changed since %s" % short
            return
        if not own:
            self.since_intersects = "false"
            self.since_reason = "the work item's own diff is empty: its affected set is empty"
            return
        if ".polyforge/verify-scope.conf" in upstream:
            self.since_hits = [".polyforge/verify-scope.conf"]
            self.since_reason = ("the upstream since %s changes .polyforge/verify-scope.conf,"
                                 " the bar's own declaration" % short)
            return
        if self.conf_closed:
            self.since_reason = "a carries or smoke_suite key of the conf is empty or malformed; failing closed"
            return
        if self.analyzer == "":
            self.since_reason = ("no analyzer (stack %s): the affected sets cannot be computed; failing closed"
                                  % (self.stack or "unknown"))
            return
        # a side whose affected set is everything meets any change of the
        # other
        path, why = self.since_side_all(own)
        if path is not None:
            self.since_hits = [path]
            self.since_reason = "the work item's affected set is the whole repository (%s)" % why
            return
        path, why = self.since_side_all(upstream)
        if path is not None:
            self.since_hits = [path]
            self.since_reason = "the upstream since %s affects the whole repository (%s)" % (short, why)
            return
        sq_why = ""
        both = sorted(set(own) & set(upstream))
        if both:
            self.since_hits += both
            sq_why = "paths both change"
        if self.analyzer == "go":
            if not self.go_graph():
                self.since_reason = "%s; failing closed" % self.go_graph_why
                return
            aff: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
            harness: Dict[str, bool] = {}
            for side, paths in (("t", own), ("u", upstream)):
                mapped = self.go_map_dirs(paths)
                closure = self.go_closure(mapped, with_harness=True)
                tr_dirs, tr_harness = self.since_textrefs(paths)
                aff[side] = sorted(set(closure) | set(tr_dirs))
                h = tr_harness
                if self.go_harness != "" and any(p.startswith(self.go_harness_dir + "/") for p in paths):
                    h = True
                harness[side] = h
            pk = sorted(set(aff["t"]) & set(aff["u"]))
            if pk:
                self.since_hits += pk
                sq_why = ("%s; " % sq_why if sq_why else "") + "packages both reach (%s)" % join_pipe(pk)
            if harness["t"] and harness["u"]:
                self.since_hits.append(self.go_harness)
                sq_why = ("%s; " % sq_why if sq_why else "") + "both reach the declared go_harness %s" % self.go_harness
        else:
            # the declarations must be sound for either side's selection to
            # count
            suites = set(self.cr_suites)
            structurally_bad = bool(self.cr_maperr) or bool(self.cr_unres) or any(
                st in ("undeclared", "malformed") for f, st in self.cr_status if f in suites)
            if structurally_bad:
                self.since_reason = "the carries declarations are structurally broken; failing closed"
                return
            sel: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
            uncov: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
            for side, paths in (("t", own), ("u", upstream)):
                sel[side], uncov[side] = self.cr_select(paths)
            if uncov["t"]:
                self.since_hits = [uncov["t"][0]]
                self.since_reason = ("the work item's affected set is the whole repository (%s is covered"
                                     " by no carries declaration)" % uncov["t"][0])
                return
            if uncov["u"]:
                self.since_hits = [uncov["u"][0]]
                self.since_reason = ("the upstream since %s affects the whole repository (%s is covered"
                                     " by no carries declaration)" % (short, uncov["u"][0]))
                return
            su = sorted(set(sel["t"]) & set(sel["u"]))
            if su:
                self.since_hits += su
                sq_why = ("%s; " % sq_why if sq_why else "") + "suites both select (%s)" % join_pipe(su)
        self.since_hits = sorted(set(self.since_hits))
        if self.since_hits:
            self.since_reason = "the upstream since %s touches the work item's affected set: %s" % (short, sq_why)
            return
        self.since_intersects = "false"
        self.since_reason = ("the upstream since %s (%d paths) touches nothing of the work item's affected set"
                             % (short, len(upstream)))

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The report.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def add_cmd(self, phase: str, cmd: str) -> None:
        self.commands.append(["", phase, "default", cmd])

    def add_conf_cmds(self, phase: str, records: Sequence[Tuple[str, str]]) -> None:
        for name, cmd in records:
            self.commands.append([name, phase, "conf", cmd])

    def name_commands(self) -> None:
        """Name the analyzer-default commands <phase>-<i> in report order. A
        phase's conf lines replace its defaults, so the two never share a
        phase."""
        counts: Dict[str, int] = {}
        for rec in self.commands:
            if rec[0] == "":
                counts[rec[1]] = counts.get(rec[1], 0) + 1
                rec[0] = "%s-%d" % (rec[1], counts[rec[1]])

    def line_report(self) -> str:
        out = ["mode: %s" % self.mode]
        for p in self.changed:
            out.append("path: %s" % p)
        for rec in self.commands:
            out.append("command: %s" % rec[3])
        if self.since_set:
            out.append("since: %s intersects=%s" % (self.since_head, self.since_intersects))
            for p in self.since_hits:
                out.append("since-path: %s" % p)
            out.append("since-reason: %s" % self.since_reason)
        return "\n".join(out) + "\n"

    def json_report(self) -> str:
        def bound(records: Sequence[Tuple[str, ...]]) -> Dict[str, List[str]]:
            out: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
            for rec in records:
                vals = out.setdefault(rec[0], [])
                if rec[1] not in vals:
                    vals.append(rec[1])
            return out

        req_env = bound(self.conf_req_env)
        req_cmd = bound(self.conf_req_cmd)
        # An undeclared timeout is null: the consumer's own default applies.
        timeouts = {k: v[0] for k, v in bound(self.conf_timeout).items()}
        # A declared failure report is {format, path}; undeclared is null.
        reports: Dict[str, Dict[str, str]] = {}
        for name, fmt, path in self.conf_report:
            reports.setdefault(name, {"format": fmt, "path": path})
        reruns = {k: v[0] for k, v in bound(self.conf_rerun).items()}
        commands = []
        for name, phase, source, cmd in self.commands:
            commands.append({
                "name": name,
                "phase": phase,
                "cmd": cmd,
                "requires_env": req_env.get(name, []),
                "requires_cmd": req_cmd.get(name, []),
                "timeout": timeouts.get(name),
                "report": reports.get(name),
                "rerun": reruns.get(name),
                "source": source,
            })
        report = {
            "schema": 1,
            "root": self.root,
            "stack": self.stack,
            "analyzer": self.analyzer or None,
            "conf": self.conf_state,
            "conf_changed_in_diff": self.conf_changed == "true",
            "base": self.base_sha or None,
            "head": self.head_sha or None,
            "default_branch": self.default_branch,
            "mode": self.mode,
            "paths": list(self.changed),
            "commands": commands,
            "test_glob": list(self.conf_globs),
            "base_setup": list(self.conf_base_setup),
            "runner": self.conf_runner or None,
            "e2e_runner": self.conf_e2e_runner or None,
            "suite_glob": list(self.conf_suite_glob),
            "e2e_map": list(self.conf_e2e_map),
            "diagnostics": list(self.diag_lines),
        }
        # An opted-in repository (any smoke_suite= line) reports its smoke
        # suites and the full-tier selection (null when none was
        # attempted); a repository that declares none keeps the report's
        # key set unchanged.
        if self.smoke_declared:
            report["smoke_suite"] = list(self.conf_smoke)
            report["e2e_selection"] = {
                "mode": self.sel_mode,
                "suites": list(self.sel_suites),
                "uncovered_paths": list(self.sel_uncov),
                "full_commands": list(self.sel_full),
            } if self.sel_mode else None
        # A repository that declares micro_path= or micro_check= (and every
        # --micro report) echoes the micro declaration; one that declares
        # neither keeps the report's key set unchanged.
        micro_checks = [cmd for _, cmd in self.conf_micro]
        if self.micro or self.conf_micro_path or micro_checks:
            report["micro_paths"] = list(self.conf_micro_path)
            report["micro_checks"] = micro_checks
        # A repository that declares an accepted host_requires= line gets
        # its host prerequisites, in file order.
        if self.conf_host:
            report["host_requires"] = [{"name": n, "probe": p, "hint": h} for n, p, h in self.conf_host]
        # --light (and only then) marks the light tier's report.
        if self.light:
            report["light"] = True
        # --since <rev> (and only then) answers whether <rev>..HEAD touches
        # this diff's affected set; any failure to compute it answered
        # intersects: true.
        if self.since_set:
            report["since"] = {
                "head": self.since_head,
                "intersects": self.since_intersects == "true",
                "paths": list(self.since_hits),
                "reason": self.since_reason,
            }
        return json.dumps(report, indent=2, ensure_ascii=True) + "\n"

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # --run.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def run_missing(self, name: str) -> str:
        """A command's missing declared prerequisites (an unset or empty
        variable, an executable not on PATH), comma-separated."""
        missing: List[str] = []
        for rn, rv in self.conf_req_env:
            if rn != name:
                continue
            if os.environ.get(rv, "") == "":
                missing.append("env %s" % rv)
        for rn, rv in self.conf_req_cmd:
            if rn != name:
                continue
            if shutil.which(rv) is None:
                missing.append("command %s" % rv)
        return ", ".join(missing)

    def run_commands(self) -> int:
        if self.micro:
            if not self.conf_micro_path:
                sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: --micro --run: the repository declares no micro_path"
                                 " at the diff base\n")
                return 1
            if not self.commands:
                sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: --micro --run: no fast-phase or micro_check command to run\n")
                return 1
        for name, _, _, cmd in self.commands:
            missing = self.run_missing(name)
            if missing != "":
                if self.micro:
                    sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: --micro --run: %s is missing %s"
                                     " (a skipped micro check is a failure): %s\n" % (name, missing, cmd))
                    return 1
                sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: skipped %s (missing %s): %s\n" % (name, missing, cmd))
                continue
            c = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", cmd], cwd=self.root,
                               stdout=sys.stderr, stderr=sys.stderr, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            if c.returncode != 0:
                sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: reported command failed: %s\n" % cmd)
                return 1
        return 0

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The main flow, in the shell engine's order.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------

    def main(self) -> int:
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="verify-scope.") as tmp:
            self.tmp = tmp
            conf = os.path.join(self.root, ".polyforge", "verify-scope.conf")
            # --micro: the governing conf is the one at the diff base (the
            # default base or --base), never the working tree's — a change
            # cannot widen its own declaration. Absent or unreadable
            # there = nothing declared.
            if self.micro:
                mbase = self.base_arg
                if mbase == "":
                    mdefault = self.diff_base_branch()
                    if mdefault is None:
                        mbase = ""
                    else:
                        c = self.proc(["git", "merge-base", "HEAD", "origin/" + mdefault], cwd=self.root)
                        mbase = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() if c.returncode == 0 else ""
                base_conf = os.path.join(tmp, "base_conf")
                ok = False
                if mbase != "":
                    c = self.proc(["git", "show", mbase + ":./.polyforge/verify-scope.conf"], cwd=self.root)
                    if c.returncode == 0:
                        with open(base_conf, "wb") as f:
                            f.write(c.stdout)
                        ok = True
                if ok:
                    conf = base_conf
                else:
                    self.diag("--micro: no .polyforge/verify-scope.conf is readable at the diff base%s;"
                              " the repository declares nothing" % ((" " + mbase) if mbase else ""))
                    conf = ""
            if conf != "" and os.path.isfile(conf):
                self.read_conf(conf)
            self.conf_req_env = self.conf_bind("check_requires_env", self.conf_req_env)
            self.conf_req_cmd = self.conf_bind("check_requires_cmd", self.conf_req_cmd)
            self.conf_timeout = self.conf_bind("check_timeout", self.conf_timeout)
            self.conf_report = self.conf_bind("report", self.conf_report)
            self.conf_rerun = self.conf_bind("rerun", self.conf_rerun)

            # go_harness is the repo's declared out-of-process acceptance
            # harness value (reported as `sh <value>`); go_harness_dir is
            # the directory whose Go packages it owns.
            if self.conf_harness:
                self.go_harness = self.conf_harness[0]
                self.go_harness_dir = path_dir(self.go_harness)

            # Affected paths of base..HEAD.
            if not self.inside_work_tree():
                self.diag("not a git work tree; falling back to the full tier")
                self.full = 1
            else:
                c = self.proc(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-prefix"], cwd=self.root)
                if c.returncode != 0:
                    self.diag("cannot resolve the engine root's place in the git work tree;"
                             " falling back to the full tier")
                    self.full = 1
                else:
                    self.root_prefix = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip("\n")
                    default = self.diff_base_branch()
                    self.default_branch = default or ""
                    c = self.proc(["git", "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD^{commit}"], cwd=self.root)
                    self.head_sha = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() if c.returncode == 0 else ""
                    if self.base_arg != "":
                        self.base = self.base_arg
                    else:
                        c = self.proc(["git", "merge-base", "HEAD", "origin/" + self.default_branch], cwd=self.root)
                        self.base = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() if c.returncode == 0 else ""
                        if self.base == "":
                            self.diag("cannot resolve the base (merge-base HEAD origin/%s);"
                                     " falling back to the full tier" % self.default_branch)
                    if self.base != "":
                        c = self.proc(["git", "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", self.base + "^{commit}"], cwd=self.root)
                        self.base_sha = c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() if c.returncode == 0 else ""
                    if self.base == "":
                        self.full = 1
                    else:
                        changed = self.diff_paths(self.base)
                        if changed is None:
                            self.diag("cannot diff %s..HEAD; falling back to the full tier" % self.base)
                            self.full = 1
                        else:
                            self.changed = changed
                            self.diff_ok = True

            # A changed path outside a nested engine root: no declaration of
            # this root can account for it, so the tier falls back to full
            # (the full-tier e2e selection, below, to the full tail). Under
            # --light the enclosing root's own run covers it: diagnosed and
            # dropped from the affected paths.
            if self.root_prefix != "":
                self.outside = [p for p in self.changed if p.startswith("../")]
                for p in self.outside:
                    if self.light:
                        self.diag("--light: changed path %s is outside the engine root (%s of the git work tree);"
                                 " the enclosing root's run covers it" % (p, self.root_prefix.rstrip("/")))
                    else:
                        self.diag("changed path %s is outside the engine root (%s of the git work tree);"
                                 " falling back to the full tier" % (p, self.root_prefix.rstrip("/")))
                        self.full = 1
                if self.light and self.outside:
                    self.changed = [p for p in self.changed if not p.startswith("../")]

            # conf_changed answers whether the diff changed the conf: the
            # conf is among the affected paths, or it differs between the
            # base and HEAD.
            if ".polyforge/verify-scope.conf" in self.changed:
                self.conf_changed = "true"
            elif self.base_sha != "" and self.head_sha != "":
                c = self.proc(["git", "diff", "--quiet", self.base_sha, self.head_sha,
                              "--", ".polyforge/verify-scope.conf"], cwd=self.root)
                if c.returncode != 0:
                    self.conf_changed = "true"

            # --full forces the full tier; it never narrows anything.
            if self.force_full:
                self.full = 1
            # --micro computes no tier (its commands are the fast phase
            # plus the micro checks), so the scoped analysis is skipped.
            if self.micro:
                self.full = 1
            # An empty or malformed carries key fails closed whatever the
            # diff.
            if self.conf_closed:
                self.full = 1

            # Stack detection. Conf first: with no analyzer the report is
            # the full tier with the conf's commands only, so the
            # no-analyzer diagnostics name what the report carries.
            if self.conf_fast or self.conf_full:
                conf_only = "the full tier with the conf's commands only"
            else:
                conf_only = "the full tier with no commands (the conf declares none)"
            if self.conf_stack:
                self.stack = self.conf_stack[0]
                if self.stack == "none":
                    self.diag("stack=none pins no analyzer; %s" % conf_only)
                else:
                    self.analyzer = self.stack
            else:
                # One supported manifest selects its analyzer; two or more
                # without a stack= pin are ambiguous.
                found: List[str] = []
                found_names: List[str] = []
                if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "go.mod")):
                    found.append("go")
                    found_names.append("go.mod")
                if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "package.json")):
                    found.append("npm")
                    found_names.append("package.json")
                if (os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "pyproject.toml"))
                        or os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "requirements.txt"))):
                    found.append("python")
                    found_names.append("pyproject.toml/requirements.txt")
                if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(self.root, "Cargo.toml")):
                    found.append("rust")
                    found_names.append("Cargo.toml")
                if not found:
                    self.stack = "unsupported"
                    self.diag("no supported stack manifest (go.mod, package.json, pyproject.toml,"
                             " requirements.txt or Cargo.toml); falling back to %s" % conf_only)
                elif len(found) > 1:
                    self.stack = "ambiguous"
                    self.diag("several stack manifests (%s) are present without a stack= pin;"
                             " falling back to %s" % (", ".join(found_names), conf_only))
                else:
                    self.stack = found[0]
                    self.analyzer = found[0]

            # The npm analyzer reads the scripts package.json actually
            # declares. Node is present wherever npm is; if it cannot read
            # the manifest, the scope cannot be computed and the report
            # fails closed with no commands.
            if self.analyzer == "npm":
                if shutil.which("node") is not None:
                    node_script = (
                        'const fs = require("fs");\n'
                        'const p = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(process.argv[1], "utf8"));\n'
                        'const s = p && p.scripts && typeof p.scripts === "object" ? p.scripts : {};\n'
                        'for (const k of Object.keys(s)) {\n'
                        '  if (typeof s[k] === "string") console.log(k + "\\t" + s[k]);\n'
                        '}\n'
                    )
                    try:
                        c = self.proc(["node", "-e", node_script, os.path.join(self.root, "package.json")],
                                     cwd=self.root)
                    except OSError:
                        c = None
                    if c is not None and c.returncode == 0:
                        self.npm_scripts = []
                        for ln in c.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n"):
                            if ln == "":
                                continue
                            name, _, cmd = ln.partition("\t")
                            self.npm_scripts.append((name, cmd))
                    if not self.npm_scripts and (c is None or c.returncode != 0):
                        self.diag("cannot read package.json scripts; falling back to %s" % conf_only)
                        self.analyzer = ""
                else:
                    self.diag("cannot read package.json scripts; falling back to %s" % conf_only)
                    self.analyzer = ""

            # No analyzer: always the full tier, with the conf's commands
            # only (the forced-full triggers and the scoped selection need
            # an analyzer).
            if self.analyzer == "":
                self.full = 1

            # Forced-full triggers: stack-generic config files plus the
            # repo's globs. --light: the conf's own triggers (forced_full=,
            # the module_root= spanning rule) force nothing — each one that
            # would have is diagnosed — while the analyzer's config files
            # still do.
            def spans_multiple_roots() -> bool:
                """Whether the changed paths touch two or more module
                roots. With a conf the roots are its module_root= list;
                otherwise each path's first-level directory is its root,
                and prose/doc paths never count."""
                roots: Set[str] = set()
                if self.conf_roots:
                    for p in self.changed:
                        if p == "" or self.path_excluded(p):
                            continue
                        for r in self.conf_roots:
                            if p == r or p.startswith(r + "/"):
                                roots.add(r)
                else:
                    for p in self.changed:
                        if p == "" or self.path_excluded(p) or self.is_doc_path(p):
                            continue
                        roots.add(p.split("/", 1)[0] if "/" in p else ".")
                return len(roots) >= 2

            if self.full == 0:
                for p in self.changed:
                    if p == "":
                        continue
                    if self.light:
                        if self.path_forced_stack(p):
                            self.diag("--light: changed path %s is the %s analyzer's own config file,"
                                     " which it cannot scope; falling back to the full tier" % (p, self.analyzer))
                            self.full = 1
                            break
                        if self.path_forced_conf(p):
                            self.diag("--light: changed path %s matches a forced_full= glob,"
                                     " which does not apply to the light tier" % p)
                        continue
                    if self.path_forced(p):
                        self.full = 1
                        break
            if self.full == 0 and self.analyzer == "go" and spans_multiple_roots():
                if self.light:
                    self.diag("--light: the diff spans two or more module_root= roots,"
                             " which does not force the full tier on the light tier")
                else:
                    self.full = 1

            # The carries layer runs for every analyzer that has it, before
            # the tier is decided (the full tail and the full-tier e2e
            # selection read the suite set).
            if self.analyzer in ("npm", "python", "rust"):
                self.cr_list_files()
                if self.analyzer == "python":
                    self.py_scan()
                if self.analyzer == "rust":
                    self.rs_scan()
                self.carries_discover()

            self.mode = "full"
            if self.full == 0:
                if not self.changed:
                    # Empty diff: scoped with an empty affected set, fast
                    # gates only.
                    self.mode = "scoped"
                elif self.analyzer == "go":
                    if self.compute_scoped_go():
                        self.mode = "scoped"
                    else:
                        self.full = 1
                elif self.analyzer == "npm":
                    if self.compute_scoped_npm():
                        self.mode = "scoped"
                    else:
                        self.full = 1
                elif self.analyzer == "python":
                    if self.compute_scoped_python():
                        self.mode = "scoped"
                    else:
                        self.full = 1
                elif self.analyzer == "rust":
                    if self.compute_scoped_rust():
                        self.mode = "scoped"
                    else:
                        self.full = 1

            # Commands: fast gates first, then the scoped selection or the
            # full tail.
            fast_scripts: Set[str] = set()
            if self.conf_fast:
                self.add_conf_cmds("fast", self.conf_fast)
            elif self.analyzer == "go":
                self.add_cmd("fast", "GOWORK=off go build ./...")
                self.add_cmd("fast", "GOWORK=off go vet ./...")
                self.add_cmd("fast", 'test -z "$(gofmt -l .)"')
            elif self.analyzer == "npm":
                if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(self.root, "node_modules")):
                    self.add_cmd("fast", "npm ci")
                if self.npm_has_script("lint"):
                    self.add_cmd("fast", "npm run lint")
                    fast_scripts.add("lint")
                if self.npm_has_typescript():
                    self.add_cmd("fast", "node_modules/.bin/tsc --noEmit")
                if self.npm_has_script("test"):
                    self.add_cmd("fast", "npm test")
                    fast_scripts.add("test")
            elif self.analyzer == "python":
                self.add_cmd("fast", "python -m compileall -q")
            elif self.analyzer == "rust":
                self.add_cmd("fast", "cargo build --all-targets")

            if self.micro:
                self.mode = "micro"
                self.add_conf_cmds("micro", self.conf_micro)
                if not self.conf_micro_path:
                    self.diag("--micro: the conf at the diff base declares no micro_path"
                             " (the repository is not opted in)")
            elif self.mode == "full":
                fast_count = len(self.commands)
                if self.conf_full:
                    self.add_conf_cmds("full", self.conf_full)
                elif self.analyzer == "go":
                    self.add_cmd("full", "GOWORK=off go test -count=1 ./...")
                elif self.analyzer == "npm":
                    script_suites = self.npm_script_suites()
                    suite_refs = {ref[len("e2e/"):] for _, ref in script_suites}
                    # Report each declared script that runs an e2e/*.mjs
                    # suite; the fast-gate scripts already ran and are not
                    # repeated.
                    for name in sorted({n for n, _ in script_suites}):
                        if name in fast_scripts:
                            continue
                        self.add_cmd("full", "npm run %s" % name)
                    # Remaining suites (the carries layer's suite set: no
                    # imported helper, no exclude= match, no e2e_runner=
                    # target), not already run by a script.
                    for s in self.cr_suites:
                        if s.startswith("e2e/") and s[len("e2e/"):] in suite_refs:
                            continue
                        self.add_cmd("full", self.cr_suite_cmd(s))
                elif self.analyzer == "python":
                    self.add_cmd("full", "pytest")
                elif self.analyzer == "rust":
                    self.add_cmd("full", "cargo test")
                # The full tail is the carried full e2e bar and the
                # selection's fallback.
                self.sel_full = [rec[3] for rec in self.commands[fast_count:]]
                if self.force_full and self.smoke_declared and self.changed:
                    self.full_tier_selection()
                    if self.sel_mode != "full":
                        self.commands = self.commands[:fast_count]
                        for c in self.cr_suite_cmds(self.sel_suites):
                            self.add_cmd("full", c)
                # The dependency-audit gate closes every full-tier report of
                # a repository with an analyzer: after the conf's or
                # analyzer's full tail and after the carries selection, so
                # no conf key reaches it.
                audit_cmd = self.audit_command()
                if audit_cmd != "" and self.analyzer != "":
                    self.commands.append(["audit", "full", "default", audit_cmd])
                    self.sel_full.append(audit_cmd)
            else:
                for c in self.scoped_cmds:
                    self.add_cmd("scoped", c)

            self.name_commands()

            if self.since_set:
                self.since_query()

            if self.json:
                try:
                    out = self.json_report()
                except Exception as err:  # a fault of the report is exit 1
                    sys.stderr.write("verify-scope: cannot emit the JSON report: %s\n" % err)
                    return 1
                sys.stdout.write(out)
            else:
                sys.stdout.write(self.line_report())

            if self.run:
                return self.run_commands()
            return 0


def main(argv: List[str]) -> int:
    return Engine(argv).main()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
